// spec: RT-001
//
// Write and WriteLn take any number of values, rendered and run together with
// nothing between them [RT-001] -- what Pascal has always done.
//
// ⚠️ THE BLANK LINE IS NOT A SPECIAL CASE. 'WriteLn ()' used to be a second
// form the native selected by arity; rendering the whole argument list gives ''
// for no arguments, which is exactly the newline on its own. The many-value
// form falls out of the same line rather than being added beside it.

WriteLn ();
WriteLn ('ABC');
WriteLn ('ABC', 123);

// Every kind of value renders as Str renders it, so the one-value form and the
// many cannot disagree.
WriteLn ('a', 'b', 'c', 1, 2.5, True, Nil);

// ⚠️ Text concatenation and not addition: this writes '12', not '3'.
WriteLn (1, 2);

Write ('no');
Write ('newline', ' here');
WriteLn ();
